\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What This Shows}

The experiment demonstrates that a small neural network can learn to allocate computational effort dynamically. The fast pathway handles easy, familiar inputs. The deliberate pathway handles novel inputs. The monitor learns when to switch between them based on episodic memory mismatch.

Most importantly, ACP achieves the same accuracy as Always Deliberate while using less compute. This proves the core claim: adaptive computation can match fixed-deliberate performance without wasting effort on easy inputs.

\subsection{Limitations}

The episodic memory is fixed and hand-designed. A real system would need to learn to write to and read from memory over time. The task is regression on random vectors, not natural language. The deliberate pathway is just another MLP, not a true reasoning module that searches or verifies. Future work should replace it with iterative refinement, tree search, or a verifier that checks answers against constraints.

The training uses supervised routing labels. In a real deployment, the system would not know which inputs are hard in advance. It would need to learn routing from task performance alone, perhaps with reinforcement learning or a confidence-based interrupt.

The easy and hard deliberation rates are nearly identical (0.642 vs 0.652), showing that the current task is too easy to elicit strong difficulty-dependent routing. This is a limitation of the synthetic task, not of the architecture.

\subsection{Toward Human-Like AI}

Humans do not think for minutes on every question. They answer quickly when the situation is familiar. They slow down when something feels off. They explain why they are thinking more. Our architecture supports this pattern. The fast pathway answers by default. The deliberate pathway engages when memory signals novelty. The routing flag can be exposed to the user as an explanation: ``I thought more about this because it did not match my previous experience.''

This is a small step toward AI that behaves less like a robot and more like a human: fluent by default, deliberate when necessary.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented Adaptive Cognitive Processing, a minimal architecture for input-dependent computation. A fast pathway answers by default. A monitor checks episodic memory and triggers a deliberate pathway when the input is novel. We implemented the system in NumPy on a CPU and showed that it matches Always Deliberate accuracy while reducing average compute by 35 percent on a synthetic task with three difficulty levels.

The code is open source at \url{https://github.com/MenteE-s/adaptive-cognitive-processing}. We hope this work contributes to the broader effort of building AI systems that allocate computation rationally, rather than applying the same effort to every input.

Future work will scale this architecture to language tasks, learn the episodic memory end-to-end, use harder tasks that elicit difficulty-dependent routing, and test the system in embodied robotics where computation must be balanced against real-time constraints.

